const QUESTIONS_DATADOG_APM=[{"id":"apm001","question":"In Datadog APM, what is a trace?","options":["The end-to-end journey of a request, represented as a tree of one or more spans","A single timed operation, such as one database query or HTTP call","A reserved tag used to group telemetry by environment","A rolling 15-minute window of raw, unsampled span data"],"answers":["The end-to-end journey of a request, represented as a tree of one or more spans"],"explanation":"A trace tracks the time spent by an application processing a single request, and is composed of one or more spans organized into a parent-child tree. The trace shows the full distributed path of the request across services. Each individual unit of work within that trace is a span.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm002","question":"What does a single span represent in Datadog APM?","options":["A complete request as it flows across every service","A named, timed unit of work such as a function call or a database query, with a start time and duration","A reserved primary tag applied to all of a service's telemetry","An aggregated dashboard widget showing p95 latency"],"answers":["A named, timed unit of work such as a function call or a database query, with a start time and duration"],"explanation":"A span represents a single logical unit of work over a period of time, such as one HTTP handler invocation or one SQL query. Each span has a name (operation name), a start timestamp, a duration, and tags/metadata. Spans link together via parent-child relationships to form a trace.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm003","question":"In Datadog APM terminology, what is a 'resource'?","options":["A physical host or container that runs the instrumented application","The Datadog Agent process that receives traces on port 8126","A particular action handled by a service, such as a specific endpoint (GET /users/{id}) or a SQL query, grouped under a span name","A saved view in the Trace Explorer"],"answers":["A particular action handled by a service, such as a specific endpoint (GET /users/{id}) or a SQL query, grouped under a span name"],"explanation":"A resource is a particular domain-specific action that a service performs, such as a canonical web endpoint or a normalized database query. For a web service, resources are typically the routes (for example GET /users/{id}); for a database service, resources are the queries grouped under a span name like db.query. Resources let you analyze performance per endpoint within a service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm004","question":"What is a 'service' in Datadog APM?","options":["A single timed operation within a request","A saved monitor that alerts on latency","A reserved tag whose default value is none","A named set of processes that do the same job, grouping together the endpoints, queries, or jobs they handle for scaling and monitoring"],"answers":["A named set of processes that do the same job, grouping together the endpoints, queries, or jobs they handle for scaling and monitoring"],"explanation":"A service is a named set of processes that perform the same task on behalf of your application, such as a web frontend, an API, or a database. Services are the building blocks of microservice architectures and group together the resources (endpoints, queries, jobs) they serve. The service name is set via DD_SERVICE under unified service tagging.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm005","question":"What is the primary rationale for adopting APM and distributed tracing in a microservices environment?","options":["To follow a single request as it propagates across many services so you can pinpoint where latency and errors originate","To replace infrastructure host monitoring entirely","To reduce the number of microservices in production","To store application source code for version control"],"answers":["To follow a single request as it propagates across many services so you can pinpoint where latency and errors originate"],"explanation":"APM with distributed tracing lets you follow one request end to end as it crosses service boundaries, so you can see exactly which service, resource, or downstream call contributes latency or errors. In a microservices architecture a single user request may touch dozens of services, and tracing stitches those calls into one view via a propagated trace ID. This makes root-cause analysis far faster than examining isolated service logs.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm006","question":"How is a distributed trace propagated across an HTTP call from one service to another?","options":["The full trace tree is serialized into the response body","Trace context such as the trace ID and parent span ID is injected into and extracted from HTTP headers so the downstream span links to the upstream trace","Each service writes its spans to a shared database that the Agent polls","The Datadog Agent broadcasts the trace ID over UDP to all hosts"],"answers":["Trace context such as the trace ID and parent span ID is injected into and extracted from HTTP headers so the downstream span links to the upstream trace"],"explanation":"Distributed tracing works by propagating trace context (trace ID, parent span ID, and sampling priority) through request headers. The upstream tracer injects these headers; the downstream tracer extracts them and creates child spans that share the same trace ID. This header propagation is what stitches spans from different services into one continuous trace.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm007","question":"Which three reserved tags make up Datadog's unified service tagging?","options":["host, container, pod","team, owner, tier","env, service, version","trace_id, span_id, parent_id"],"answers":["env, service, version"],"explanation":"Unified service tagging ties metrics, traces, and logs together using three reserved tags: env, service, and version. These are set through the DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, and DD_VERSION environment variables (or equivalent config). Using them consistently lets you correlate telemetry and navigate seamlessly between traces, logs, and metrics for the same service and deployment.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm008","question":"What is the role of the 'env' tag in unified service tagging?","options":["It identifies the deployed code version and changes with every release","It records the operation name of each span","It names the set of processes doing the same job","It is the mandatory primary tag identifying the environment (such as prod or staging) that traces are collected from, defaulting to env:none when unset"],"answers":["It is the mandatory primary tag identifying the environment (such as prod or staging) that traces are collected from, defaulting to env:none when unset"],"explanation":"The env tag is the default and mandatory primary tag that scopes telemetry by environment, such as prod, staging, or dev. When data is not tagged with an environment, Datadog assigns env:none. Scoping by env lets you compare and isolate the same service across environments.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm009","question":"Why does the 'version' tag matter for unified service tagging?","options":["It is expected to change with each new deployment so you can compare performance and errors between code versions","It must remain constant for the life of a service so dashboards stay stable","It is the only tag the Agent uses to route traces","It defines the latency threshold T for Apdex"],"answers":["It is expected to change with each new deployment so you can compare performance and errors between code versions"],"explanation":"The version tag (DD_VERSION) is expected to change with each application deployment, so two different builds carry distinct version values. This enables deployment tracking: you can compare error rates and latency across versions and quickly spot a regression introduced by a new release. It is the foundation of Datadog's version-over-version and canary comparison views.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm010","question":"In Datadog APM, how many services can a single span belong to?","options":["Up to four services depending on tags","Exactly one service per span","As many services as it has child spans","None; spans are not associated with services"],"answers":["Exactly one service per span"],"explanation":"There can only be one service per span. Each span is attributed to exactly one service, which is why a downstream call appears as a new span owned by the downstream service. This one-to-one mapping is what lets Datadog roll span metrics up cleanly to the owning service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm011","question":"What distinguishes a Datadog out-of-the-box (OOTB) tracer from a community/third-party tracer in Datadog APM?","options":["OOTB tracers cannot send data to the Datadog Agent","Community tracers are always faster and officially supported by Datadog","OOTB Datadog tracing libraries are built and maintained by Datadog with first-class auto-instrumentation, while community tracers are third-party (often OpenTelemetry-based) integrations","There is no difference; both are identical builds"],"answers":["OOTB Datadog tracing libraries are built and maintained by Datadog with first-class auto-instrumentation, while community tracers are third-party (often OpenTelemetry-based) integrations"],"explanation":"Datadog provides official out-of-the-box tracing libraries (dd-trace) for major languages, with maintained auto-instrumentation for popular frameworks and direct support. Community or third-party tracers, such as OpenTelemetry SDKs, can also send trace data to Datadog but are not built by Datadog and may differ in coverage and feature parity. The exam expects you to know the OOTB Datadog tracer offers the deepest, first-class integration.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm012","question":"Why do automatic instrumentation capabilities differ between programming languages in Datadog APM?","options":["Datadog charges more for certain languages so it limits features","Auto-instrumentation is identical in every language","Only compiled languages can be traced at all","Each language and runtime exposes different hooks, bytecode, or monkey-patching mechanisms, so the depth of auto-instrumentation varies by language"],"answers":["Each language and runtime exposes different hooks, bytecode, or monkey-patching mechanisms, so the depth of auto-instrumentation varies by language"],"explanation":"Language-level differences exist because each runtime offers different mechanisms for hooking into libraries, such as bytecode manipulation in Java, monkey-patching in Python and Ruby, or import hooks in Node.js. As a result, the set of frameworks supported out of the box and the depth of automatic instrumentation varies by language. Candidates should know auto-instrumentation coverage is language-dependent, and manual instrumentation can fill gaps.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm013","question":"Which tracing architecture does Datadog APM use to collect spans from an application?","options":["The tracing library in the application sends spans to a local Datadog Agent, which forwards them to the Datadog backend","Applications send spans directly to Datadog's SaaS backend over the public internet, bypassing any local agent","Spans are written to local log files that an external service scrapes","The Datadog backend polls each application for spans on a schedule"],"answers":["The tracing library in the application sends spans to a local Datadog Agent, which forwards them to the Datadog backend"],"explanation":"In Datadog's standard architecture, the in-process tracing library generates spans and sends them to a local Datadog Agent, typically over HTTP on port 8126. The Agent buffers, applies sampling, and forwards the trace data to Datadog's backend. This decouples the application from the network path to Datadog and centralizes sampling and tagging.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm014","question":"After spans are ingested, how long does Datadog retain spans that are indexed by a custom retention filter?","options":["15 minutes","15 days","24 hours","13 months"],"answers":["15 days"],"explanation":"Spans that match a custom retention filter are indexed and stored for 15 days, making them searchable and available for analytics over that window. This is distinct from Live Search, which shows all ingested spans for only the last 15 minutes. Indexing through retention filters is what makes specific traces queryable for two weeks.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm015","question":"For how long are all ingested spans available in Live Search before retention filters decide what to index?","options":["The last 60 minutes","The last 15 days","The last 15 minutes, in a rolling window, with no sampling applied","Only spans matching a retention filter are ever shown in Live Search"],"answers":["The last 15 minutes, in a rolling window, with no sampling applied"],"explanation":"Live Search displays all ingested spans as soon as the Agent sends them, for a rolling window of the last 15 minutes, without sampling. This lets you investigate very recent activity, including spans that retention filters may not ultimately index. After 15 minutes, only spans kept by retention filters remain searchable (for 15 days).","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm016","question":"What is the purpose of tags on spans in Datadog APM?","options":["They permanently delete spans that no longer match a filter","They replace the need for a trace ID","They are required to start the Datadog Agent","They add key-value metadata (such as http.status_code, error type, or customer_id) so spans can be searched, filtered, grouped, and correlated"],"answers":["They add key-value metadata (such as http.status_code, error type, or customer_id) so spans can be searched, filtered, grouped, and correlated"],"explanation":"Tags are key-value metadata attached to spans that enrich them with context such as HTTP status, error details, host, or business identifiers. They power search, filtering, faceting, and grouping in the Trace Explorer and let you correlate traces with logs and metrics. Unified service tags (env, service, version) are reserved tags that tie all telemetry together.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm017","question":"Which statement best describes Datadog's approach to APM compared with traditional sampling-heavy tools?","options":["Datadog ingests traces and lets you live-search recent spans, retain only the traces that matter via retention filters, while computing trace metrics from 100% of traffic","Datadog drops all traces and keeps only aggregate metrics","Datadog requires you to manually instrument every line of code","Datadog only supports a single programming language"],"answers":["Datadog ingests traces and lets you live-search recent spans, retain only the traces that matter via retention filters, while computing trace metrics from 100% of traffic"],"explanation":"Datadog's approach separates ingestion, retention, and metrics. All ingested spans are live-searchable for 15 minutes, retention filters decide which traces to index and keep for 15 days, and trace metrics (request count, error count, latency) are computed from 100% of traffic regardless of sampling. This gives complete metric accuracy while controlling the storage cost of full traces.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm018","question":"What is the relationship between a parent span and a child span within a trace?","options":["The child span always belongs to a different trace than the parent","The child span is work performed during the parent's execution, sharing the same trace ID and referencing the parent's span ID","Parent and child spans must belong to different environments","A child span has no duration of its own"],"answers":["The child span is work performed during the parent's execution, sharing the same trace ID and referencing the parent's span ID"],"explanation":"Within a single trace, a parent span calls into work that produces child spans; each child shares the trace ID and records its parent's span ID. This parent-child linkage builds the tree structure visualized in the flame graph. A downstream service call, a database query, or an internal function can all become child spans of the operation that invoked them.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm019","question":"On which default port does the Datadog Agent receive traces from tracing libraries over HTTP?","options":["8125","443","8126","5000"],"answers":["8126"],"explanation":"The Datadog Agent's trace-agent component listens for traces on TCP port 8126 by default. Tracing libraries send their spans to this endpoint (commonly http://localhost:8126), and the Agent then forwards them to Datadog. Port 8125 is the default for DogStatsD metrics, which is a common distractor.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm020","question":"Why are Datadog APM trace metrics (hits, errors, latency) considered accurate even when trace ingestion is sampled?","options":["Because sampling is never applied to any application","Because only indexed spans contribute to trace metrics","Because the metrics are estimated from a 1% sample and then extrapolated","Because trace metrics are computed from 100% of the application's traffic at the Agent/tracer before ingestion sampling drops full traces"],"answers":["Because trace metrics are computed from 100% of the application's traffic at the Agent/tracer before ingestion sampling drops full traces"],"explanation":"Trace metrics such as request count, error count, and latency are calculated from 100% of an application's traffic, independent of any ingestion sampling that reduces the number of full traces stored. The tracer/Agent computes these metrics on every request, so dashboards and monitors built on them remain accurate even when most full traces are dropped. This is a core advantage of Datadog's sampling model.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm021","question":"What does an 'operation name' (span name) typically represent in Datadog APM?","options":["A general category of the work a span does, such as web.request, db.query, or http.request, under which specific resources are grouped","The unique business identifier of an end user","The 15-day retention timestamp of a span","The environment the span was collected in"],"answers":["A general category of the work a span does, such as web.request, db.query, or http.request, under which specific resources are grouped"],"explanation":"The operation name (also called span name) describes the kind of work a span represents at a high level, for example web.request for an incoming HTTP request or db.query for a database call. Specific resources, such as individual endpoints or queries, are grouped beneath a span name. This grouping keeps cardinality manageable while still letting you drill into individual resources.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm022","question":"Which best describes the difference between ingested spans and indexed spans in Datadog APM?","options":["Ingested spans are stored forever; indexed spans are deleted immediately","Ingested spans are all spans the Agent sends to Datadog (live-searchable 15 minutes); indexed spans are the subset kept by retention filters and stored 15 days for search and analytics","Indexed spans are sent to the Agent; ingested spans never leave the host","They are two names for exactly the same thing"],"answers":["Ingested spans are all spans the Agent sends to Datadog (live-searchable 15 minutes); indexed spans are the subset kept by retention filters and stored 15 days for search and analytics"],"explanation":"Ingested spans are all spans the tracer/Agent forwards to Datadog and are available in Live Search for the last 15 minutes without sampling. Indexed spans are the subset that match a retention filter and are therefore stored for 15 days, making them available for historical search and trace analytics. Understanding this distinction is central to controlling cost and ensuring the right traces are retained.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm023","question":"Which environment variable sets the service name a Datadog tracer reports for unified service tagging?","options":["DD_ENV","DD_VERSION","DD_SERVICE","DD_TRACE_AGENT_PORT"],"answers":["DD_SERVICE"],"explanation":"DD_SERVICE sets the service name reported by the tracer and is one of the three unified service tagging variables. DD_ENV sets the environment and DD_VERSION sets the deployed version. Setting all three consistently is what enables correlation across traces, logs, and metrics for the same service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm024","question":"A request enters a web service, which calls an authentication service, which queries a database. In Datadog APM, how is this represented?","options":["As three separate traces, one per service","As three resources with no spans","As a single span containing all three services","As a single trace whose spans span all three services, linked by a shared trace ID propagated between them"],"answers":["As a single trace whose spans span all three services, linked by a shared trace ID propagated between them"],"explanation":"Because trace context is propagated as the request crosses each service boundary, all of the work appears in one distributed trace that contains spans from the web service, the auth service, and the database call. They share the same trace ID, and the flame graph shows the parent-child structure across services. This single-trace view is the core value of distributed tracing.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm025","question":"What happens to trace data tagged with no environment under unified service tagging?","options":["It is automatically assigned env:none so it still appears, but is not scoped to a real environment","It is rejected by the Agent and never ingested","It is duplicated into every environment","It is stored for 13 months as long-term metrics"],"answers":["It is automatically assigned env:none so it still appears, but is not scoped to a real environment"],"explanation":"When trace data lacks an environment value, Datadog assigns the default env:none. The data is still collected and visible, but it is grouped under env:none rather than a meaningful environment like prod or staging. Setting DD_ENV avoids this and ensures telemetry is correctly scoped for filtering and correlation.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"APM fundamentals"},{"id":"apm026","question":"What is the name of Datadog's family of out-of-the-box tracing libraries used to instrument applications?","options":["dd-agent","dd-trace","dd-statsd","dd-logs"],"answers":["dd-trace"],"explanation":"Datadog's official tracing libraries are collectively known as dd-trace, with language-specific implementations such as dd-trace-java, dd-trace-py, dd-trace-js, dd-trace-go, and dd-trace-rb. These libraries provide automatic instrumentation for popular frameworks and APIs for custom spans. They send the resulting spans to the local Datadog Agent.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm027","question":"What does automatic instrumentation provide when you enable a Datadog tracing library in a supported framework?","options":["It requires you to wrap every function manually with start_span calls","It only collects infrastructure metrics, not traces","It instruments supported libraries and frameworks for you, generating spans for web requests, database calls, and external HTTP calls without code changes","It replaces the application's web server"],"answers":["It instruments supported libraries and frameworks for you, generating spans for web requests, database calls, and external HTTP calls without code changes"],"explanation":"Automatic instrumentation patches supported libraries and frameworks so the tracer emits spans for common operations, such as incoming web requests, database queries, cache calls, and outbound HTTP, with little or no code change. This gives broad visibility quickly, and you can add custom spans where you need deeper detail. It is the fastest way to get most applications traced.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm028","question":"When would you add custom (manual) instrumentation in addition to automatic instrumentation?","options":["Never; custom spans are not allowed in Datadog APM","When you want to disable the Datadog Agent","Only when using an unsupported operating system","When you want to trace in-house business logic or code paths not covered by a supported library"],"answers":["When you want to trace in-house business logic or code paths not covered by a supported library"],"explanation":"Custom instrumentation lets you create spans around in-house code or business logic that auto-instrumentation does not cover, giving deeper visibility into specific functions. You can also add tags to existing spans or set the resource name. Auto-instrumentation handles common frameworks, while custom spans fill the gaps that matter to your application.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm029","question":"What does Datadog's Single Step Instrumentation aim to do?","options":["Automatically install and configure the Datadog tracing SDK and begin capturing traces with a single command and no code changes","Force you to edit application code for every span","Only enable log collection","Disable distributed tracing across services"],"answers":["Automatically install and configure the Datadog tracing SDK and begin capturing traces with a single command and no code changes"],"explanation":"Single Step Instrumentation installs and configures the Datadog SDK and starts auto-instrumentation in one command, so supported applications begin emitting traces without code changes. It is designed to make onboarding fast across hosts, containers, or Kubernetes. You can still layer custom spans on top afterward.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm030","question":"In Datadog's standard architecture, where does the tracing library send spans?","options":["Directly to a Datadog dashboard URL","To the local Datadog Agent (typically localhost:8126), which forwards them to the Datadog backend","To a community message queue that the user must run","To the application's own database"],"answers":["To the local Datadog Agent (typically localhost:8126), which forwards them to the Datadog backend"],"explanation":"The in-process tracing library batches spans and sends them to the local Datadog Agent, usually over HTTP at localhost:8126. The Agent applies sampling and tagging, then securely forwards the data to Datadog's backend. This Agent-mediated path is the standard Datadog APM architecture.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm031","question":"What is head-based sampling in Datadog's trace ingestion?","options":["The decision to keep or drop a trace is made after the full trace completes, based on its outcome","Only spans with errors are ever ingested","The decision to keep or drop a trace is made at the first (head) span when the trace is created, then propagated to downstream services","Sampling is performed exclusively in the Datadog backend after storage"],"answers":["The decision to keep or drop a trace is made at the first (head) span when the trace is created, then propagated to downstream services"],"explanation":"Head-based sampling makes the keep/drop decision at the very first span of a trace, when the trace begins, and propagates that sampling priority downstream so the whole trace is treated consistently. This keeps complete traces intact rather than fragments. Datadog also applies additional Agent-side sampling mechanisms to catch errors and diverse latencies that head-based sampling might miss.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm032","question":"By default, what target throughput does the Datadog Agent aim for when applying automatic head-based sampling to a high-traffic application?","options":["1000 traces per second per host","Exactly 1 trace per minute per Agent","100% of all traces regardless of traffic","About 10 complete traces per second per Agent"],"answers":["About 10 complete traces per second per Agent"],"explanation":"For high-traffic applications, the Agent lowers the sampling rate toward a default target of roughly 10 complete traces per second per Agent to control ingestion volume. Low-traffic applications are sampled at 100% so you do not lose visibility. This adaptive behavior balances cost and coverage automatically.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm033","question":"What is the difference between sampling and retention in Datadog APM?","options":["Sampling (ingestion) controls which traces are sent to Datadog; retention filters control which of the ingested spans are indexed and stored for 15 days","They are identical concepts with two names","Retention happens in the application; sampling happens in the database","Sampling stores spans for 15 days; retention keeps them for 15 minutes"],"answers":["Sampling (ingestion) controls which traces are sent to Datadog; retention filters control which of the ingested spans are indexed and stored for 15 days"],"explanation":"Sampling (ingestion control) determines which traces leave the Agent and reach Datadog, reducing volume close to the source. Retention filters then decide which ingested spans are indexed and kept for 15 days for search and analytics. Trace metrics remain computed on 100% of traffic, so both stages affect stored traces but not metric accuracy.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm034","question":"Which capability lets you prevent sensitive data, such as query parameters or PII, from appearing in trace data?","options":["There is no way to scrub trace data in Datadog APM","APM data security features such as obfuscation of database statements and configurable scrubbing/redaction of span tags and resource names","Increasing the sampling rate to 100%","Disabling the Datadog Agent entirely"],"answers":["APM data security features such as obfuscation of database statements and configurable scrubbing/redaction of span tags and resource names"],"explanation":"Datadog provides APM data security controls, including automatic obfuscation of SQL and other database statements and configurable scrubbing or redaction of span tags, resource names, and query parameters. These features keep secrets and PII out of stored traces. You configure them in the tracer and Agent rather than by changing sampling.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm035","question":"What is required to correlate application logs with traces so you can pivot from a span to its related logs in Datadog?","options":["Storing logs and traces in the same file on disk","Disabling unified service tagging","Injecting trace and span IDs (such as dd.trace_id and dd.span_id) into log entries, and using consistent service, env, and version tags","Sending logs only to the application console"],"answers":["Injecting trace and span IDs (such as dd.trace_id and dd.span_id) into log entries, and using consistent service, env, and version tags"],"explanation":"Trace/log correlation works by injecting the trace ID and span ID into log records (for example dd.trace_id and dd.span_id), so Datadog can link a log line to the exact span that produced it. Consistent unified service tags (service, env, version) make the pivot seamless in both directions. This lets you jump from a slow or errored span straight to the relevant logs.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm036","question":"What does connecting RUM (Real User Monitoring) to APM traces enable?","options":["It disables backend tracing in favor of browser metrics","It stores traces for 13 months","It replaces unified service tagging","It links a user's browser/mobile session and actions to the backend traces they triggered, giving end-to-end visibility from frontend to backend"],"answers":["It links a user's browser/mobile session and actions to the backend traces they triggered, giving end-to-end visibility from frontend to backend"],"explanation":"Connecting RUM and traces ties a frontend user action (page load, click, route change) to the backend distributed traces it generates, so you can follow a real user's request from the browser all the way through your services. This end-to-end view helps you see how backend latency or errors affect actual user experience. It is enabled by propagating trace context from the RUM SDK into backend requests.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm037","question":"What does Datadog Continuous Profiler add on top of distributed tracing?","options":["It collects code-level profiles (CPU, memory, lock, wall time) so you can see which methods and lines consume resources, complementing trace-level latency","It replaces spans with logs","It only measures network bandwidth","It disables flame graphs"],"answers":["It collects code-level profiles (CPU, memory, lock, wall time) so you can see which methods and lines consume resources, complementing trace-level latency"],"explanation":"The Continuous Profiler captures code-level profiling data such as CPU time, allocations/memory, lock contention, and wall-clock time across your production code. While traces show which spans are slow, profiles show which specific methods or lines inside those spans consume the resources. Together they take you from a slow endpoint to the exact hot code path.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm038","question":"What does Deployment Tracking in Datadog APM rely on to compare performance before and after a release?","options":["The host name only","The version tag (DD_VERSION), which changes per deployment so Datadog can attribute traces and metrics to specific code versions","The trace ID of the first request","The Apdex threshold T"],"answers":["The version tag (DD_VERSION), which changes per deployment so Datadog can attribute traces and metrics to specific code versions"],"explanation":"Deployment Tracking uses the version tag set by DD_VERSION, which changes with each deployment, to group telemetry by code version. This lets Datadog show error rate and latency version-over-version and surface regressions introduced by a new release. Canary and rolling deployment views are built on this version attribution.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm039","question":"Which set of environment variables is the recommended way to configure unified service tagging for a Datadog-traced application?","options":["DD_API_KEY, DD_SITE, DD_HOSTNAME","DD_TRACE_ENABLED, DD_LOGS_ENABLED, DD_PROFILING_ENABLED","DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, DD_VERSION","DD_AGENT_HOST, DD_TRACE_AGENT_PORT, DD_DOGSTATSD_PORT"],"answers":["DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, DD_VERSION"],"explanation":"Unified service tagging is configured with DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, and DD_VERSION, which set the env, service, and version reserved tags on traces (and StatsD metrics). Applying these consistently ties traces, logs, and metrics together. The other variables control different aspects such as API keys, feature toggles, or Agent connectivity.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm040","question":"Which instrumentation types are available with Datadog tracing libraries?","options":["Only fully manual instrumentation","Only compile-time instrumentation","Only agentless instrumentation with no library","Automatic instrumentation of supported libraries, plus optional custom (manual) instrumentation for code not covered automatically"],"answers":["Automatic instrumentation of supported libraries, plus optional custom (manual) instrumentation for code not covered automatically"],"explanation":"Datadog supports automatic instrumentation, which patches supported frameworks and libraries to emit spans without code changes, and custom (manual) instrumentation, where you create spans and add tags around your own logic via the library API. Most teams start with auto-instrumentation for broad coverage and add custom spans where needed. The two approaches are complementary.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm041","question":"Within the Datadog Agent, which component is responsible for receiving and processing traces?","options":["The trace-agent (APM Agent) component","The DogStatsD server","The process-agent","The security-agent"],"answers":["The trace-agent (APM Agent) component"],"explanation":"The trace-agent, also called the APM component of the Datadog Agent, receives spans from tracing libraries on port 8126, applies sampling and obfuscation, computes trace metrics, and forwards traces to Datadog. DogStatsD handles custom metrics, while process-agent and security-agent serve other purposes. Knowing the trace-agent's role is part of understanding the Agent architecture.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm042","question":"How does Datadog typically obfuscate SQL database statements captured in spans?","options":["It deletes all database spans","It automatically replaces literal values in SQL with placeholders so individual queries are grouped and sensitive values are not stored","It encrypts the entire trace with a customer key","It refuses to instrument databases"],"answers":["It automatically replaces literal values in SQL with placeholders so individual queries are grouped and sensitive values are not stored"],"explanation":"Datadog automatically obfuscates SQL statements by replacing literal values with placeholders (for example, turning specific IDs or strings into ?), which both protects sensitive data and normalizes many concrete queries into a single resource. This keeps query cardinality manageable and prevents secrets in literals from being stored. It is a default part of APM data security for database integrations.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm043","question":"What is the benefit of injecting trace context into outbound requests when instrumenting a service?","options":["It increases the sampling rate automatically","It encrypts the request payload","It allows the downstream service to continue the same trace, preserving end-to-end visibility across service boundaries","It removes the need for a Datadog Agent"],"answers":["It allows the downstream service to continue the same trace, preserving end-to-end visibility across service boundaries"],"explanation":"Injecting trace context (trace ID, parent span ID, sampling decision) into outbound requests lets the receiving service extract it and create child spans under the same trace. This preserves the distributed trace across service boundaries so the flame graph shows the full request path. Without propagation, each service would produce disconnected traces.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm044","question":"Which statement about adding custom span tags during manual instrumentation is correct?","options":["Custom tags are forbidden and rejected by the Agent","Custom tags can only be numeric","Custom tags overwrite the trace ID","You can attach custom key-value tags (for example customer.tier or order.id) to spans to enrich search, filtering, and correlation"],"answers":["You can attach custom key-value tags (for example customer.tier or order.id) to spans to enrich search, filtering, and correlation"],"explanation":"During manual instrumentation you can set custom tags on the active or current span, such as customer.tier or order.id, to add business context. These tags become facets you can search, filter, and group by in the Trace Explorer, and they help correlate traces with specific conditions. They supplement, and never overwrite, the trace and span identifiers.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm045","question":"In a Kubernetes environment, what is a common way to set unified service tags for an instrumented pod?","options":["Set DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, and DD_VERSION as environment variables (often from pod labels) on the application container","Hard-code them in the Datadog backend UI for each pod","They cannot be set in Kubernetes","Only by editing the Datadog Agent's API key"],"answers":["Set DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, and DD_VERSION as environment variables (often from pod labels) on the application container"],"explanation":"In Kubernetes you typically set DD_ENV, DD_SERVICE, and DD_VERSION as container environment variables, frequently sourced from standard pod labels (such as tags.datadoghq.com/env). This ensures each pod's traces, logs, and metrics carry consistent unified service tags. Doing so enables correlation and deployment tracking across the cluster.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm046","question":"What does the Datadog Lambda extension enable for serverless tracing?","options":["It blocks tracing in AWS Lambda","It lets AWS Lambda functions send traces (and metrics/logs) to Datadog without running a separate host Agent","It only works for EC2 instances","It replaces the application code in the function"],"answers":["It lets AWS Lambda functions send traces (and metrics/logs) to Datadog without running a separate host Agent"],"explanation":"The Datadog Lambda extension runs alongside your function as a Lambda layer/extension and forwards traces, metrics, and logs to Datadog without needing a traditional host-based Agent. This brings APM and unified service tagging to serverless functions. It is the standard way to instrument AWS Lambda with Datadog.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm047","question":"Why is consistent use of the service tag across an application important for instrumentation?","options":["It changes the Apdex score automatically","It increases the trace retention period to 30 days","It ensures each set of processes is grouped under one stable service name so metrics, traces, and logs roll up to the correct service","It is only needed for the Agent to start"],"answers":["It ensures each set of processes is grouped under one stable service name so metrics, traces, and logs roll up to the correct service"],"explanation":"A consistent service tag (DD_SERVICE) ensures all telemetry from the same set of processes rolls up under one service name, giving accurate service-level metrics, clean Service Map nodes, and correct trace/log correlation. Inconsistent or duplicated service names fragment the data and confuse dashboards. It is a foundational part of unified service tagging.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm048","question":"What is OpenTelemetry's relationship to Datadog APM?","options":["Datadog cannot ingest any OpenTelemetry data","OpenTelemetry is the only supported tracer for Datadog","OpenTelemetry replaces the Datadog backend","Datadog can ingest traces produced by OpenTelemetry SDKs/Collector as a community/third-party tracing path, in addition to its own dd-trace libraries"],"answers":["Datadog can ingest traces produced by OpenTelemetry SDKs/Collector as a community/third-party tracing path, in addition to its own dd-trace libraries"],"explanation":"Datadog supports OpenTelemetry as a third-party/community tracing option: applications instrumented with OpenTelemetry SDKs or routed through the OpenTelemetry Collector can send trace data into Datadog. This sits alongside the out-of-the-box dd-trace libraries, which offer the deepest first-class integration. Candidates should know both paths exist and how they differ.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm049","question":"Which additional Agent-side sampling mechanisms help ensure important traces are kept even when head-based sampling would drop them?","options":["Error and rare/diverse sampling that retain traces with errors and a representative variety of resources and latencies","Random deletion of all error traces","Sampling that keeps only the fastest requests","Sampling that ignores latency entirely"],"answers":["Error and rare/diverse sampling that retain traces with errors and a representative variety of resources and latencies"],"explanation":"Beyond head-based sampling, the Agent applies error sampling and rare/diverse sampling to capture traces with errors and a representative spread of resources and latency profiles that would otherwise be dropped. This ensures you do not lose visibility into failures or uncommon code paths. It is part of how Datadog retains the most useful traces while controlling volume.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm050","question":"When you set DD_VERSION on every deployment, which Datadog capability becomes most useful?","options":["Live Search of the last 15 minutes","Deployment tracking and version-over-version comparison of errors and latency","DogStatsD metric ingestion","Database query obfuscation"],"answers":["Deployment tracking and version-over-version comparison of errors and latency"],"explanation":"Setting DD_VERSION per deployment powers deployment tracking, letting Datadog compare error rates and latency across versions and highlight regressions immediately after a release. It also underpins canary deployment analysis. Without changing the version tag, Datadog cannot attribute behavior to a specific code version.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Application instrumentation"},{"id":"apm051","question":"Which Datadog APM view lets you search, filter, and analyze individual spans and traces in real time?","options":["The Service Map","The Software Catalog only","The Trace Explorer","The Notebook editor"],"answers":["The Trace Explorer"],"explanation":"The Trace Explorer is where you search and analyze traces and spans using facets, tags, and free-text search, then pivot to related logs and metrics. It supports both Live Search of recent ingested spans and search over indexed spans. It is the primary investigation surface for finding specific traces during troubleshooting.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm052","question":"In the Trace Explorer search syntax, how do you find spans for a specific service?","options":["By typing the service name with no key, such as just web-store","By using span_id:web-store","By querying retention_filter:web-store","By using a facet/tag filter like service:web-store"],"answers":["By using a facet/tag filter like service:web-store"],"explanation":"Datadog search uses key:value syntax over facets and tags, so service:web-store filters spans to that service. You can combine multiple conditions, such as service:web-store status:error, and use boolean operators. Free-text and wildcard searches are also supported, but scoping by the service facet is the canonical approach.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm053","question":"Which search query returns only error spans for the checkout service?","options":["service:checkout status:error","service:checkout status:ok","service:checkout -status:error","resource:checkout error:false"],"answers":["service:checkout status:error"],"explanation":"Combining facets with key:value pairs, service:checkout status:error returns spans from the checkout service that are marked as errors. The status facet distinguishes ok versus error spans. This kind of compound query is the standard way to isolate failing requests for a service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm054","question":"What does the Datadog APM service page primarily show for a selected service?","options":["The raw Agent configuration file","Key health metrics such as request rate, error rate, latency (including percentiles), plus resources, dependencies, and version comparisons","Only the billing usage for that service","A list of all users who accessed the service"],"answers":["Key health metrics such as request rate, error rate, latency (including percentiles), plus resources, dependencies, and version comparisons"],"explanation":"The service page surfaces a service's golden signals, request throughput, error rate, and latency percentiles (p50/p75/p90/p95/p99), along with its top resources, upstream/downstream dependencies, and the ability to compare versions during deployments. From there you can drill into traces for problematic resources. It is the hub for monitoring a single service's performance.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm055","question":"What does a flame graph in a Datadog trace visualize?","options":["The CPU temperature of the host","The number of users on the site","The hierarchy and timing of spans within a trace, showing which services, endpoints, and queries contributed latency and how they nest","The Agent's memory usage over time"],"answers":["The hierarchy and timing of spans within a trace, showing which services, endpoints, and queries contributed latency and how they nest"],"explanation":"A flame graph renders the spans of a single trace as nested, time-proportional bars, so you can see the parent-child structure, which operations ran in sequence or parallel, and where time was spent. Wider bars indicate longer-running spans, making slow services, endpoints, or database calls easy to spot. It is the primary way to read an individual trace.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm056","question":"In a flame graph, what does the width of a span bar generally represent?","options":["The number of tags on the span","The alphabetical order of the service name","The retention period of the span","The duration of that span (how long the operation took)"],"answers":["The duration of that span (how long the operation took)"],"explanation":"In a Datadog flame graph the horizontal width of each span is proportional to its duration, so longer operations appear as wider bars. This makes it immediate to identify the spans that dominate a request's total time. Drilling into the widest spans is the fastest route to a latency root cause.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm057","question":"What is the difference between Live Search and indexed (retained) search in the Trace Explorer?","options":["Live Search queries all ingested spans from the last 15 minutes with no sampling; indexed search queries spans kept by retention filters over the last 15 days","Live Search covers 15 days; indexed search covers 15 minutes","Both query the same 15-day window","Indexed search only works on the Agent, Live Search only in the cloud"],"answers":["Live Search queries all ingested spans from the last 15 minutes with no sampling; indexed search queries spans kept by retention filters over the last 15 days"],"explanation":"Live Search lets you query every ingested span from the last 15 minutes without sampling, useful for very recent, ad hoc investigation. Indexed (retained) search queries the subset of spans that retention filters kept, which are stored for 15 days, supporting historical analysis. Choosing the right mode depends on how recent and how complete you need the data to be.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm058","question":"What are facets in the Datadog Trace Explorer?","options":["Pre-built dashboards shipped by Datadog","Indexed span attributes/tags (such as service, resource_name, http.status_code) that you can filter, group, and aggregate by","A type of monitor that pages on-call","The Agent's sampling rules"],"answers":["Indexed span attributes/tags (such as service, resource_name, http.status_code) that you can filter, group, and aggregate by"],"explanation":"Facets are span attributes and tags promoted into searchable, aggregatable dimensions, such as service, env, resource_name, http.status_code, or custom tags. They let you filter the trace stream, group results, and build analytics and visualizations. Core facets exist by default, and you can create custom facets from any span tag.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm059","question":"On a service page, how would you identify the slowest endpoints (resources) of a service?","options":["By reading the Agent log file","By checking the host's disk usage","By sorting the resources list by latency (for example p95) to surface the highest-latency endpoints","By exporting the entire trace database"],"answers":["By sorting the resources list by latency (for example p95) to surface the highest-latency endpoints"],"explanation":"The resources list on a service page can be sorted by latency metrics such as p95 or p99, request count, or error rate, letting you quickly find the slowest endpoints. From a slow resource you can open representative traces and their flame graphs to find the root cause. This top-down, resource-first workflow is central to insight discovery.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm060","question":"What does the Span Summary (span aggregation) view help you do?","options":["Delete spans older than 15 days","Render a single trace's flame graph only","Configure the Agent's API key","Aggregate many spans by attributes to see counts, latency distributions, and patterns across a group of spans rather than one trace at a time"],"answers":["Aggregate many spans by attributes to see counts, latency distributions, and patterns across a group of spans rather than one trace at a time"],"explanation":"The span summary/aggregation capability groups spans by chosen attributes (such as resource or status) and shows aggregate measures like count, error rate, and latency percentiles. This reveals patterns across thousands of spans rather than forcing you to inspect individual traces. It is useful for spotting which resources or tag values dominate latency or errors.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm061","question":"How does Datadog APM help you group similar errors instead of facing a noisy stream of individual error spans?","options":["Error Tracking aggregates similar errors into issues so you can assess impact and prioritize the most frequent or highest-impact problems","By deleting all error spans automatically","By converting errors into Apdex scores","By disabling error spans in production"],"answers":["Error Tracking aggregates similar errors into issues so you can assess impact and prioritize the most frequent or highest-impact problems"],"explanation":"Error Tracking groups similar errors (by stack trace, type, and message) into issues, turning a noisy stream into a manageable list ranked by frequency and impact. You can see which resources have the most issues and track an issue's status over time. This makes triage far more efficient than scanning raw error spans.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm062","question":"After a deployment, which Datadog APM feature shows whether a new version increased latency or errors compared with the previous version?","options":["Live Search of the last 15 minutes","Deployment Tracking, using the version tag to compare versions side by side","The DogStatsD port configuration","The Agent's flare diagnostic"],"answers":["Deployment Tracking, using the version tag to compare versions side by side"],"explanation":"Deployment Tracking uses the version tag to compare error rate and latency between the previous and new versions, so a regression introduced by a release is immediately visible. You can view version-over-version graphs and identify the offending deployment. This relies on DD_VERSION changing with each release.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm063","question":"What does the Profiler's flame graph show that a trace flame graph does not?","options":["The same span-by-span request timeline","The list of unified service tags","Code-level hot paths (which methods/lines consume CPU, memory, or lock time) aggregated from profiling samples","The Agent's network ports"],"answers":["Code-level hot paths (which methods/lines consume CPU, memory, or lock time) aggregated from profiling samples"],"explanation":"A profiler flame graph aggregates code-level samples to show which methods and call stacks consume CPU, allocate memory, or hold locks, independent of any single request. A trace flame graph instead shows the spans of one specific request over time. Together they connect a slow endpoint to the exact functions burning resources.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm064","question":"Which search would find spans whose HTTP status code is 500 across all services?","options":["version:500","status:500","resource:500","http.status_code:500"],"answers":["http.status_code:500"],"explanation":"HTTP status codes are captured on spans under the http.status_code tag, so http.status_code:500 returns spans that responded with HTTP 500 regardless of service. You can combine it with a service filter to narrow further. The status facet (status:error) is separate and reflects span error state, not the HTTP code.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm065","question":"From a single trace's flame graph, what can you typically pivot to for additional context?","options":["Correlated logs, host/container metrics, related spans, and (if enabled) profiles tied to that request","Nothing; the flame graph is a dead end","Only the billing page","Only the Agent installation guide"],"answers":["Correlated logs, host/container metrics, related spans, and (if enabled) profiles tied to that request"],"explanation":"From a span in a flame graph you can pivot to correlated logs (via injected trace IDs), the host or container metrics for the time of the request, related traces, and code profiles where the Profiler is enabled. This in-context navigation lets you move from a slow or errored span straight to the supporting evidence. Correlation across telemetry is a core strength of Datadog APM.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm066","question":"Which boolean operator usage correctly finds spans for the cart service that are NOT errors?","options":["service:cart AND status:error","service:cart AND -status:error","service:cart OR status:error","service:cart status:error"],"answers":["service:cart AND -status:error"],"explanation":"Datadog search supports boolean operators and the minus sign for negation, so service:cart AND -status:error (equivalently service:cart -status:error) returns cart spans that are not errors. AND requires both conditions, while the leading minus excludes the error condition. OR would broaden rather than narrow the results.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm067","question":"What is the role of the env (environment) facet when discovering insights in the Trace Explorer?","options":["It sets the retention period of spans","It increases the sampling rate","It scopes search and analytics to a particular environment (such as prod or staging) so you analyze the right population of traffic","It renames the service"],"answers":["It scopes search and analytics to a particular environment (such as prod or staging) so you analyze the right population of traffic"],"explanation":"The env facet lets you scope queries and analytics to a specific environment, so you can analyze prod traffic separately from staging or dev. Because env is a unified primary tag, it is a reliable filter across traces, logs, and metrics. Scoping by environment avoids mixing test traffic into production analysis.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm068","question":"Which view would you use to inspect the upstream and downstream dependencies of a single service?","options":["The Agent flare","The billing dashboard","The DogStatsD configuration","The service page dependency map (and the global Service Map)"],"answers":["The service page dependency map (and the global Service Map)"],"explanation":"A service's dependencies, the services that call it and the services it calls, are shown on the service page's dependency view and in the global Service Map. These let you understand the blast radius of a problem and trace latency that originates upstream or downstream. They are built from the aggregate call relationships in your traces.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm069","question":"When investigating a recent spike that occurred two minutes ago, which search mode is best suited to see every span without sampling?","options":["Live Search, which shows all ingested spans from the last 15 minutes","Indexed (retained) search over 15 days","The Service Map","A 13-month metrics query"],"answers":["Live Search, which shows all ingested spans from the last 15 minutes"],"explanation":"For something that happened in the last few minutes, Live Search is ideal because it shows all ingested spans from the rolling 15-minute window without any sampling, so you do not miss spans that retention filters might drop. Indexed search is better for historical analysis over 15 days but only includes retained spans. Choosing Live Search ensures complete recent coverage.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm070","question":"What information does selecting a single span within a flame graph reveal?","options":["Only the service name","The span's duration, operation name, resource, tags/metadata, and any errors or stack trace attached to it","The Agent's CPU usage","The number of indexed spans in the account"],"answers":["The span's duration, operation name, resource, tags/metadata, and any errors or stack trace attached to it"],"explanation":"Clicking a span exposes its full detail: start time and duration, operation and resource names, all span tags and metadata, and error information such as the message and stack trace when present. This is how you confirm exactly what a particular operation did and why it was slow or failed. It connects the high-level flame graph to ground-truth span data.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm071","question":"Which capability lets you turn a Trace Explorer query into a saved, reusable metric for dashboards and monitors?","options":["Live Search","Obfuscation","Generating custom span-based (trace) metrics from indexed spans","Single Step Instrumentation"],"answers":["Generating custom span-based (trace) metrics from indexed spans"],"explanation":"Datadog lets you generate custom metrics from spans by defining a query and the dimensions/aggregations you care about, producing a span-based metric you can graph and alert on. This is useful for tracking business-specific or high-cardinality conditions over time without retaining every trace. It complements the default trace metrics computed on 100% of traffic.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm072","question":"On the service page, which signals are commonly referred to as the key performance indicators (golden signals) of a service?","options":["Disk IOPS, inode count, and swap usage","Tag count, facet count, and span name length","API key, site, and hostname","Request rate (throughput), error rate, and latency"],"answers":["Request rate (throughput), error rate, and latency"],"explanation":"Datadog APM surfaces the core service health signals: request rate (throughput), error rate, and latency (often broken out by percentile). These let you judge whether a service is healthy at a glance and spot anomalies. They are computed from trace metrics derived from 100% of traffic.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm073","question":"Why are latency percentiles (such as p95 and p99) preferred over the average when analyzing a service's latency?","options":["Percentiles reveal the tail/worst-case experience that averages hide, so you can see how slow the slowest requests are","Averages are always larger than percentiles","Percentiles ignore errors entirely","Averages cannot be computed from trace metrics"],"answers":["Percentiles reveal the tail/worst-case experience that averages hide, so you can see how slow the slowest requests are"],"explanation":"Percentiles like p95 and p99 describe the slow tail of the latency distribution, exposing how bad the worst few percent of requests are, which a mean can mask. A service can have a fine average while a meaningful fraction of users experience very slow responses. Datadog APM provides these percentiles so you can manage real user experience and SLOs.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm074","question":"What does grouping spans by resource_name in trace analytics help you discover?","options":["The Agent's installed version","Which specific endpoints or queries within a service have the most traffic, highest latency, or most errors","The number of hosts in the cluster","The retention filter configuration"],"answers":["Which specific endpoints or queries within a service have the most traffic, highest latency, or most errors"],"explanation":"Grouping by resource_name breaks a service down into its individual endpoints or queries, so you can rank them by request count, latency percentiles, or error rate. This pinpoints exactly which resource is responsible for a service-level problem. It is a common first step before opening representative traces.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm075","question":"Which statement about custom retention filters is correct?","options":["They can only filter by service name","They retain spans for only 15 minutes","They let you define, based on any span attribute or tag, which spans to index and keep for 15 days so the traces that matter to your business are retained","They disable trace metrics"],"answers":["They let you define, based on any span attribute or tag, which spans to index and keep for 15 days so the traces that matter to your business are retained"],"explanation":"Custom retention filters let you specify, using any span attribute or tag, which spans should be indexed and retained for 15 days, ensuring business-critical traces are always available for search. You can create multiple filters in addition to the always-on intelligent retention filter. This gives precise control over which traces survive beyond the live window.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Insight discovery"},{"id":"apm076","question":"What does the Datadog Service Map visualize?","options":["The source code of each service","A single trace's flame graph","Only the host-level network topology","Your services as nodes and the aggregate calls between them as edges, giving a real-time view of dependencies and health"],"answers":["Your services as nodes and the aggregate calls between them as edges, giving a real-time view of dependencies and health"],"explanation":"The Service Map renders each instrumented service as a node and the aggregate calls between services as edges, built from APM (and RUM) data. Node coloring and indicators reflect health, so you can see dependencies and where problems propagate at a glance. The nodes match the services in your Service/Software Catalog.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm077","question":"What does an edge between two nodes on the Service Map represent?","options":["Aggregate calls from one service to another (the dependency relationship and its volume)","A shared host between the services","A retention filter linking the services","A monitor that pages both teams"],"answers":["Aggregate calls from one service to another (the dependency relationship and its volume)"],"explanation":"Each edge represents the aggregate calls flowing from one service to another, reflecting the dependency and its request volume. This shows you the direction and intensity of inter-service communication. Following edges helps you trace how latency or errors ripple through the architecture.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm078","question":"What is the primary purpose of the Datadog Service Catalog (Software Catalog)?","options":["To store the application's binary artifacts","To provide a consolidated, single view of your services with ownership metadata, performance, dependencies, and related telemetry","To replace the Trace Explorer","To configure the Agent's sampling rate"],"answers":["To provide a consolidated, single view of your services with ownership metadata, performance, dependencies, and related telemetry"],"explanation":"The Service/Software Catalog gives one consolidated place to see all your services along with ownership and team metadata, performance insights, dependencies, on-call, and links to dashboards and docs. It helps teams understand who owns what and quickly reach the right context for a service. Its service entries align with the nodes on the Service Map.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm079","question":"What is a Service Performance Dashboard (APM dashboard) used for?","options":["Editing the application's source code","Storing raw spans for 13 months","Visualizing a service's key APM metrics (throughput, error rate, latency) on customizable widgets for monitoring and sharing","Replacing unified service tagging"],"answers":["Visualizing a service's key APM metrics (throughput, error rate, latency) on customizable widgets for monitoring and sharing"],"explanation":"APM/service performance dashboards present a service's golden signals, such as request rate, error rate, and latency percentiles, on widgets you can arrange, share, and combine with other telemetry. They give teams an at-a-glance health view and support investigation by linking to traces. They are built from trace metrics and can include span-based custom metrics.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm080","question":"What is the Apdex score in Datadog APM?","options":["The total number of spans ingested per second","The number of services in the Service Map","The percentage of spans that were sampled","A standardized 0-to-1 user-satisfaction score for response times, based on a latency threshold T"],"answers":["A standardized 0-to-1 user-satisfaction score for response times, based on a latency threshold T"],"explanation":"Apdex (Application Performance Index) is a standardized score from 0 to 1 that summarizes user satisfaction with response times relative to a chosen threshold T. A score near 1 means most requests are fast and satisfying; a low score means many requests are slow or failing. It gives a single, comparable health number per web service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm081","question":"In the Apdex model with threshold T, how is a request classified as 'tolerating'?","options":["Its response time is greater than T and up to 4T (between T and 4T)","Its response time is below T","Its response time is above 4T","It returned an error"],"answers":["Its response time is greater than T and up to 4T (between T and 4T)"],"explanation":"Apdex buckets requests using T: satisfied requests are faster than T, tolerating requests fall between T and 4T, and frustrated requests are slower than 4T or return an error. Tolerating requests are acceptable but not ideal, so they count as half in the score. Knowing these bands is essential for interpreting and configuring Apdex.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm082","question":"Which formula correctly computes the Apdex score?","options":["(satisfied + frustrated) / total","(satisfied + (tolerating x 0.5)) / total requests","satisfied / (tolerating + frustrated)","(tolerating + frustrated) / satisfied"],"answers":["(satisfied + (tolerating x 0.5)) / total requests"],"explanation":"Apdex equals the number of satisfied requests plus half the tolerating requests, divided by the total number of requests: (satisfied + tolerating x 0.5) / total. Frustrated requests contribute zero. This yields a value between 0 and 1 where higher is better.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm083","question":"If a web service has an Apdex threshold T of 0.3 seconds, at what response time does a request become 'frustrated' purely on latency?","options":["Above 0.3 seconds","Above 0.6 seconds","Above 1.2 seconds","Above 3.0 seconds"],"answers":["Above 1.2 seconds"],"explanation":"The frustrated threshold is 4T, so with T = 0.3 seconds a request becomes frustrated when its response time exceeds 4 x 0.3 = 1.2 seconds (or if it errors). Requests between 0.3 and 1.2 seconds are tolerating, and those under 0.3 seconds are satisfied. Recognizing the 4T relationship is key to configuring Apdex.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm084","question":"What are trace (APM) metrics, and how are they generated?","options":["Hand-written custom metrics that require code changes for every endpoint","Metrics that only exist for the Datadog Agent itself","Metrics computed only from indexed spans retained for 15 days","Metrics such as request count, error count, and latency that Datadog generates automatically from trace data on 100% of traffic"],"answers":["Metrics such as request count, error count, and latency that Datadog generates automatically from trace data on 100% of traffic"],"explanation":"Trace metrics, including hit/request count, error count, and latency, are generated automatically from trace data for service entry spans and certain operations, and they are computed from 100% of traffic regardless of ingestion sampling. This makes them accurate and reliable for dashboards, monitors, and SLOs. They exist out of the box once a service is instrumented.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm085","question":"Which tags are available by default on Datadog trace metrics for breaking them down?","options":["env, service, version, resource, http.status_code/status_class, plus host tags","Only the trace ID","Only the Agent hostname","Only custom user-defined tags"],"answers":["env, service, version, resource, http.status_code/status_class, plus host tags"],"explanation":"Trace metrics carry dimensions such as env, service, version, resource, http.status_class and http.status_code, along with host tags from the Datadog Host Agent and configured primary tags. These let you slice latency, throughput, and errors by environment, version, endpoint, or status. This rich tagging is what makes trace-metric dashboards so flexible.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm086","question":"What does configuring a custom Apdex threshold T per service let you do?","options":["Change the trace retention period","Tune what response time counts as satisfactory for that specific service, so the Apdex score reflects realistic expectations","Increase the sampling rate","Rename the service"],"answers":["Tune what response time counts as satisfactory for that specific service, so the Apdex score reflects realistic expectations"],"explanation":"Because different services have different latency expectations, Datadog lets you set the Apdex threshold T per service so the satisfied/tolerating/frustrated bands match that service's realistic performance target. A fast cache service might use a small T, while a heavy reporting service uses a larger one. This makes the Apdex score meaningful for each service.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm087","question":"Why might you create a span-based (custom) metric from trace data instead of relying on default trace metrics?","options":["To delete spans faster","To disable unified service tagging","To track a specific dimension or condition (for example a custom tag like customer.plan) over time and use it in monitors, even after spans expire","To stop the Agent from sampling"],"answers":["To track a specific dimension or condition (for example a custom tag like customer.plan) over time and use it in monitors, even after spans expire"],"explanation":"Span-based custom metrics let you persist a measurement defined by your own query and tags, such as latency grouped by customer.plan, so you can chart and alert on it long after the underlying spans age out. This is helpful for high-cardinality or business-specific KPIs that default trace metrics do not capture. The metric is computed from spans matching your filter.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm088","question":"An Apdex score of 1.0 for a service indicates what?","options":["Every request was frustrated","No traffic was received","Half the requests errored","Essentially all requests were satisfied (faster than T with no errors), reflecting excellent response-time performance"],"answers":["Essentially all requests were satisfied (faster than T with no errors), reflecting excellent response-time performance"],"explanation":"An Apdex of 1.0 means effectively all requests fell in the satisfied band, faster than the threshold T and without errors, indicating excellent performance from the user's perspective. As tolerating or frustrated requests appear, the score drops below 1. The score gives a quick, normalized read on user-perceived speed.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm089","question":"How does the Service Map help during an incident affecting multiple services?","options":["It shows health across the dependency graph so you can see how a failure in one service propagates to its callers and dependencies","It rewrites the failing code automatically","It deletes the unhealthy services","It only displays billing information"],"answers":["It shows health across the dependency graph so you can see how a failure in one service propagates to its callers and dependencies"],"explanation":"During an incident the Service Map visualizes the health of each service and the call relationships between them, so you can trace a problem from a symptomatic service back to the upstream or downstream service actually at fault. Coloring and indicators highlight unhealthy nodes and impacted paths. This dependency-aware view speeds up root-cause isolation.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm090","question":"Which is the most appropriate way to share a curated, ongoing view of several services' APM health with a team?","options":["Send a single trace ID by email","Build a service performance dashboard combining throughput, error rate, latency, and Apdex widgets","Increase the sampling rate to 100%","Disable retention filters"],"answers":["Build a service performance dashboard combining throughput, error rate, latency, and Apdex widgets"],"explanation":"A dashboard built from APM trace metrics, request rate, error rate, latency percentiles, and Apdex, gives a team a persistent, shareable view of service health that updates continuously. Widgets can be arranged per service and combined with logs and infrastructure metrics. This is far more durable than sharing a single trace or changing ingestion settings.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm091","question":"Because trace metrics are computed on 100% of traffic, what is true about dashboards built from them under aggressive ingestion sampling?","options":["The dashboards will undercount requests in proportion to the sampling rate","The dashboards stop updating","The dashboards remain accurate for throughput, errors, and latency even though fewer full traces are stored","The dashboards only show indexed spans"],"answers":["The dashboards remain accurate for throughput, errors, and latency even though fewer full traces are stored"],"explanation":"Since trace metrics are generated from all traffic before ingestion sampling drops full traces, dashboards and monitors built on those metrics stay accurate for request counts, error counts, and latency even when sampling is aggressive. Sampling reduces the number of full example traces you can inspect, not the metric accuracy. This separation is a deliberate design of Datadog's pipeline.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Visualizing insights"},{"id":"apm092","question":"During an incident, which Datadog APM workflow best helps you find example failing requests quickly?","options":["Reading the Agent's startup log","Restarting all services blindly","Changing the unified service tags","Using Trace Search in the Trace Explorer with a query like service:<svc> status:error to surface and open failing traces"],"answers":["Using Trace Search in the Trace Explorer with a query like service:<svc> status:error to surface and open failing traces"],"explanation":"Trace Search lets you query the trace stream during an incident, for example service:payments status:error, to immediately surface failing requests and open their flame graphs. From there you can read error messages, stack traces, and correlated logs to find the cause. This targeted search is far faster than scanning raw logs.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm093","question":"You see elevated latency on a service but its own code looks fast in the flame graph. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["A downstream dependency (such as a database or an external service the span calls) is slow, contributing most of the latency","The Apdex threshold is misconfigured to zero","The Agent is on the wrong port","The version tag is missing"],"answers":["A downstream dependency (such as a database or an external service the span calls) is slow, contributing most of the latency"],"explanation":"When a service's own work is quick but total latency is high, the flame graph usually shows the time being spent inside child spans for a downstream call, such as a slow database query or an external API. Following the widest downstream span pinpoints the real bottleneck. Distributed tracing is specifically designed to attribute latency to the responsible dependency.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm094","question":"What is the recommended way to be notified automatically when a service's error rate or latency crosses a threshold?","options":["Manually refresh the Trace Explorer all day","Create an APM monitor (for example on error rate, latency percentile, or Apdex) that alerts via notification channels when conditions breach","Increase trace retention to 30 days","Disable the affected service"],"answers":["Create an APM monitor (for example on error rate, latency percentile, or Apdex) that alerts via notification channels when conditions breach"],"explanation":"APM monitors let you alert on trace-metric conditions such as error rate, p95/p99 latency, throughput drops, or Apdex, and route notifications to channels like email, Slack, or PagerDuty. They run continuously so you do not have to watch dashboards manually. This is the standard way to catch regressions proactively.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm095","question":"Which type of APM monitor would alert you specifically when a service starts returning an unusually high proportion of error spans?","options":["A disk space monitor","A login-attempt monitor","An error-rate (or error-tracking) APM monitor on the service's error metric","A monitor on the Agent's CPU usage"],"answers":["An error-rate (or error-tracking) APM monitor on the service's error metric"],"explanation":"An APM monitor on the service's error rate (or one driven by Error Tracking issues) fires when the proportion or count of error spans exceeds your threshold. Because error counts are part of trace metrics computed on all traffic, the alert is accurate even under sampling. This focuses your attention on real failure spikes.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm096","question":"How does correlating traces with logs speed up troubleshooting an errored request?","options":["It removes the error from the system","It deletes related spans","It increases the sampling rate to 100%","Injected trace IDs let you jump from the errored span directly to the exact log lines emitted during that request for detailed context"],"answers":["Injected trace IDs let you jump from the errored span directly to the exact log lines emitted during that request for detailed context"],"explanation":"Because trace and span IDs are injected into logs, an errored span links directly to the precise log lines produced while handling that request, including stack traces and contextual messages. This eliminates guesswork about which logs correspond to the failure. Pivoting trace-to-logs (and back) is a core Datadog troubleshooting capability.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm097","question":"An APM monitor should page on-call only for sustained problems, not brief blips. Which configuration helps achieve this?","options":["Using an appropriate evaluation window and threshold (and optionally requiring the condition to persist) so transient spikes do not trigger alerts","Setting the evaluation window very short, like a few seconds","Disabling notifications entirely","Removing the service from the Service Catalog"],"answers":["Using an appropriate evaluation window and threshold (and optionally requiring the condition to persist) so transient spikes do not trigger alerts"],"explanation":"Choosing a reasonable evaluation window and threshold, and requiring the breach to persist, prevents noisy alerts from momentary spikes while still catching sustained degradations. Overly short windows cause flapping and alert fatigue. Tuning these settings is part of building trustworthy APM alerting.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm098","question":"After a deployment, error rate jumps for one service. Which feature most directly confirms the new version is responsible?","options":["Live Search of the last 15 minutes only","Deployment tracking comparing the new version tag against the previous version's error rate and latency","Increasing the Apdex threshold","The Agent's flare command"],"answers":["Deployment tracking comparing the new version tag against the previous version's error rate and latency"],"explanation":"Deployment tracking compares metrics by version tag, so you can see the error-rate and latency change line up exactly with the new version's rollout, confirming the regression came from that deployment. This version-over-version view is precisely what DD_VERSION enables. It points you to rolling back or fixing the offending release.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm099","question":"Which combined workflow is most effective for diagnosing intermittent slow requests on a specific endpoint?","options":["Only look at the average latency on a dashboard","Restart the Datadog Agent repeatedly","Filter the service page resource by the endpoint, sort by p99 latency, open representative slow traces, read their flame graphs, and pivot to correlated logs and profiles","Disable retention filters for that service"],"answers":["Filter the service page resource by the endpoint, sort by p99 latency, open representative slow traces, read their flame graphs, and pivot to correlated logs and profiles"],"explanation":"For intermittent slowness you isolate the resource, examine the high-percentile latency (p99) where the tail lives, then open representative slow traces and read their flame graphs to see which span is responsible, pivoting to logs and profiles for the deepest cause. Averages would hide the tail you care about. This top-down, percentile-aware investigation is the recommended troubleshooting path.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"},{"id":"apm100","question":"Why is using Trace Search during an active incident often faster than browsing logs alone?","options":["Logs are never useful in incidents","Trace Search only shows data older than 15 days","Trace Search disables the failing service automatically","Trace Search lets you filter by service, resource, status, and tags to immediately surface impacted requests and their full distributed path, then pivot to the exact related logs"],"answers":["Trace Search lets you filter by service, resource, status, and tags to immediately surface impacted requests and their full distributed path, then pivot to the exact related logs"],"explanation":"Trace Search lets you slice the live trace stream by service, resource, status, and any tag, so you instantly find the affected requests and see their end-to-end path across services in a flame graph. From there, correlation takes you to the precise logs and profiles, rather than manually grepping unrelated log volume. This targeted, distributed view shortens time-to-resolution.","sources":["open-exam-prep.com Datadog APM Specialist practice bank"],"adjudicated":false,"needs_review":false,"topic_hint":"Troubleshooting applications"}];
